  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, expect generally low impact weather across\nthe Northland. Plentiful cloud cover will linger through the\nafternoon hours, keeping temperatures on the cooler side. As we\nhead into tonight, a few light lake effect rain showers are\npossible for the Minnesota Borderlands coming off of Lake of the\nWoods with around a twenty percent chance of very spotty \nmeasurable rainfall. No notable accumulations are expected from \nthese inland lake effect rain showers, and nearly all of the \nregion will remain completely dry as overnight low temperatures \ndrop into the upper thirties to lower forties.\n\nSkies will slowly clear as we move through Wednesday, leading to\ncontinued near normal conditions across our region. High\ntemperatures will comfortably reach the middle to upper fifties as\nweak surface high pressure drifts across the area. Wednesday night\nwill feature partly cloudy skies initially before clouds begin to\nthicken once again ahead of our next approaching weather system.\n\nRain chances return to the forecast on Thursday, primarily focused\nacross northwest Wisconsin and even areas further southward into\ncentral Wisconsin outside of the Northland. Thursday night into\nearly Friday morning, a secondary Canadian low pressure system \nwill drag a cold front through the area. This setup will create\nwrap around cold air advection and will usher in gusty \nnorthwest winds Thursday night into Friday daytime, with gusts \nof 25 to 35 mph expected areawide. These winds will be \nespecially notable from the Twin Ports high terrain extending up\nthrough the entire North Shore. As the colder air filters in, a\nfew rain and snow shower mixes are possible for the Borderlands\nto Arrowhead in Minnesota early Friday morning. It is very \nlikely no snowfall even accumulates (95% chance), but if it is \nable to drop enough in a very localized area then a dusting of \nsnow is the max expected.\n\nLake effect showers are also possible Friday morning along the\nSouth Shore. These will primarily fall as rain near the\nshorelines, but pockets of inland northern Iron County cannot rule\nout a few snow showers. A substantially drier airmass rushes in by\nFriday afternoon, causing minimum relative humidity values to drop\ninto the thirty to forty percent range. Friday night will be quite\ncold, with low temperatures very likely from the low-20s to\nupper-20s across nearly all areas.\n\nVery low dewpoints will linger into Saturday daytime as\ntemperatures rebound into the upper fifties and lower sixties.\nMinimum relative humidity values will plummet into the 25-35 \npercent range, and historical trends for this similar weather\npattern suggest they could drop even lower. Combined with \nsouthwest winds sustained at 10 to 15 mph and gusting to 25 mph,\nnear critical fire weather conditions are possible Saturday mid\nday through early evening. We will be tracking these forecast\nconditions and may be in communication with Land Management \nAgencies later this week to discuss how fuels are looking for \nany kind of possible fire weather headline needs.\n\nNo rainfall is forecast this weekend into early next week as a \nwarmer than normal airmass settles in, bringing temperatures \nback into the sixties.",
